   - pH (in pH units)
   - Organic matter content (in % by weight)
   - Soil texture (e.g., clay, loam, sandy)

3. Normalize the data: To ensure that all variables contribute equally to the analysis, normalize the data to a common scale (e.g., 0-1 scale).

4. Perform principal-matrix correlation: Calculate the correlation matrix for the normalized data. This matrix will show the pairwise relationships between the variables.

5. Perform principal-matrix transformation: Perform the PCA on the correlation matrix. This will generate new variables called principal coordinate axes (PC1, PC2, PC3, etc.), which are linear combinations of the original variables. The first PC (PC1) will represent the most significant combination of variables, the second (PC2) the second most significant, and so on.

