","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔS = ΣnS(products) - ΣnS(reactants)

where n is the number of moles and S is the standard entropy of each substance.

The standard entropies (S°) for CO and CO2 at 298 K are as follows:

S°(CO) = 197.7 J/(mol·K)
S°(CO2) = 213.6 J/(mol·K)

Now, we can plug these values into the equation:

ΔS = [3 mol × 213.6 J/(mol·K)] - [3 mol × 197.7 J/(mol·K)]

ΔS = 640.8 J/K - 593.1 J/K

ΔS = 47.7 J/K

So, the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction is 47.7 J/K.
