What is the Gibbs free energy change for the electrochemical reaction:
Zn(s) + Cu2+(aq) → Zn2+(aq) + Cu(s)
given that the standard reduction potentials are E°(Cu2+|Cu) = +0.34 V and E°(Zn2+|Zn) = -0.76 V, and the reaction is carried out at 25°C?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To calculate the Gibbs free energy change (ΔG°) for the electrochemical reaction, we first need to determine the overall cell potential (E°cell) using the standard reduction potentials. The cell potential is the difference between the reduction potential of the cathode (where reduction occurs) and the reduction potential of the anode (where oxidation occurs).

In this case, the Cu2+/Cu half-cell has a higher reduction potential, so it will act as the cathode, and the Zn2+/Zn half-cell will act as the anode.

E°cell = E°(cathode) - E°(anode)
E°cell = E°(Cu2+|Cu) - E°(Zn2+|Zn)
E°cell = (+0.34 V) - (-0.76 V)
E°cell = +1.10 V

Now that we have the cell potential, we can use the following equation to calculate the Gibbs free energy change:

ΔG° = -nFE°cell

where n is the number of moles of electrons transferred in the reaction, F is the Faraday constant (96,485 C/mol), and E°cell is the cell potential.

In this reaction, 2 moles of electrons are transferred (Zn loses 2 electrons, and Cu2+ gains 2 electrons).

ΔG° = -(2 mol)(96,485 C/mol)(1.10 V)
ΔG° = -212,467 J/mol

The Gibbs free energy change for the electrochemical reaction is -212,467 J/mol.
