The coordination number of the zinc ion plays a crucial role in the catalytic activity of carbonic anhydrase. The zinc ion acts as a Lewis acid, polarizing the water molecule or hydroxide ion it is coordinated to. This polarization increases the nucleophilicity of the water molecule or hydroxide ion, allowing it to attack the carbon dioxide substrate and facilitate the conversion of carbon dioxide to bicarbonate ion.

