","When solid benzoic acid (H(C₇H₅O₂)) reacts with sodium hydroxide (NaOH), the following observable effects occur:
1. The solid benzoic acid dissolves in the aqueous NaOH solution, and the solution becomes clear.
2. The solution becomes basic (alkaline) due to the formation of hydroxide ions (OH⁻).
3. The solution may release heat due to the reaction being exothermic.

The fully ionized equation for the reaction is:
\[
\text{H(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2 (s)} + \text{NaOH (aq)} \rightarrow \text{Na(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2 (aq)} + \text{H}_2\text{O (l)}
\]
Here, the benzoic acid donates a proton (H⁺) to the hydroxide ion (OH⁻) from NaOH, forming water (H₂O) and the sodium salt of benzoic acid (Na(C₇H₅O₂)). The sodium salt is soluble in water, so the solution becomes clear. 

Final answer: $\boxed{\text{H(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2 (s)} + \text{NaOH (aq)} \rightarrow \text{Na(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2 (aq)} + \text{H}_2\text{O (l)}}$
