"In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","In molecular orbital theory, the key difference between σ molecular orbitals and π molecular orbitals lies in the electron density distribution:  
1. **σ molecular orbitals**: These orbitals are formed by the head-on (axial) overlap of atomic orbitals, resulting in electron density concentrated along the internuclear axis.  
2. **π molecular orbitals**: These orbitals are formed by the side-by-side (sidewise) overlap of atomic orbitals, resulting in electron density concentrated above and below the internuclear axis.  

Thus, σ orbitals have a single nodal plane (where electron density is zero) along the internuclear axis, while π orbitals have two nodal planes (one above and one below the internuclear axis).  

$\boxed{\text{σ orbital: electron density along the internuclear axis, one nodal plane along the axis.}}$  
$\boxed{\text{π orbital: electron density above/below the internuclear axis, two nodal planes along the axis.}}$
