","The equation of the tangent line to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \) is given by:
\[
y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a)
\]
where \( f'(a) \) is the derivative of \( f \) at \( a \). This equation represents the slope-intercept form of the tangent line. 

Thus, the slope of the tangent is \( f'(a) \), and the line passes through the point \( (a, f(a)) \). 

Final answer: \(\boxed{y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a)}\)
