   \]
2. The partial sums are increasing:
   \[
   f(2) = \frac{3}{2}, \quad f(4) > 2, \quad f(8) > \frac{5}{2}, \quad f(16) > 3, \quad f(32) > \frac{7}{2}
   \]
3. The difference between the partial sums is increasing as well.

These observations suggest that the function $f(2^n)$ is increasing and its value is approximately the sum of the reciprocals of powers of 2 up to $2^n$. 

