","(a) The Wiener process is generated by the recursion equation:
\[
X_{k+1} = X_k + W_k, \quad k = 0, 1, 2, \ldots, 10
\]
where \(X_0 = 0\) and \(W_k\) are independent \(N(0, 1)\) random variables. The sampling interval is 1 s.  

To generate 50 sample realizations, we use a random number generator to generate 50 independent sets of 11 independent standard normal variables. The recursion equation is then applied to each set to obtain the 11 values of the Wiener process at each time step. The results are stored in a 50 \(\times\) 11 matrix, where each row represents a realization.  

(b) The nonstationary character of the process can be observed by plotting the 8 realizations. The Wiener process has a variance that increases linearly with time, so the variance of the process increases as time increases. This property is characteristic of a nonstationary process.  

(c) The average squares of the 50 realizations are computed by squaring each value in the 50 \(\times\) 11 matrix and then averaging over all 50 rows. The result is a 11-dimensional vector, where each component represents the average square of the realization at a specific time step.  

The average squares are then plotted versus time. The plot should be approximately linear with a slope of unity because the variance of the Wiener process increases linearly with time.  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{The Wiener process is a stochastic process with the property that its increments are independent and identically distributed (i.i.i.f.) exponential variables.} \\
&\text{The variance of the Wiener process increases linearly with time, which means that the process is nonstationary.} \\
&\text{The average squares of the 50 realizations should be approximately linear with a slope of unity, as the variance of the Wiener process increases linearly with time.}
\end{aligned}
}
