","To determine the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a photon scattered by an electron, we use the Compton effect formula:

\[
\Delta \lambda = \lambda' - \lambda = \frac{h}{m_e c} (1 - \cos \theta)
\]

where:
- \(\Delta \lambda = \lambda' - \lambda\) is the change in wavelength,
- \(\lambda'\) is the scattered wavelength,
- \(\lambda = 2.00 \, \text{Å}\) is the initial wavelength,
- \(h\) is Planck's constant (\(6.626 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s}\)),
- \(m_e\) is the electron mass (\(9.109 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}\)),
- \(c\) is the speed of light (\(3.00 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}\)),
- \(\theta\) is the scattering angle.

The maximum change occurs when \(\cos \theta = -1\) (i.e., \(\theta = 180^\circ\)), so:

\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = \frac{2h}{m_e c}
\]

The percentage change is then:

\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}}}{\lambda} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{2h}{m_e c \lambda} \right) \times 100
\]

Substituting the values:

\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{2 \times 6.626 \times 10^{-34}}{9.109 \times 10^{-31} \times 3.00 \times 10^8 \times 2.00 \times 10^{-10}} \right) \times 100
\]

\[
\text{Percentage change} \approx 0.00025\%
\]

Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength is \(\boxed{0.00025\%}\).
