","To calculate the work done, we use the formula:

\[
W = F \cdot d \cdot \cos \theta
\]

Where:
- \( W \) is the work done,
- \( F \) is the force applied (60.0 N),
- \( d \) is the displacement (10.0 m),
- \( \theta \) is the angle between the force and displacement (60.0°).

Substituting the given values:

\[
W = 60.0 \, \text{N} \times 10.0 \, \text{m} \times \cos 60.0°
\]

Since \( \cos 60.0° = 0.5 \):

\[
W = 60.0 \times 10.0 \times 0.5 = 300 \, \text{J}
\]

Thus, the work done is \(\boxed{300 \, \text{J}}\).
