","The typical range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy is \(\boxed{\pm 1.1 \times 10^{-13} \text{ MeV}}\). This is calculated using the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle, which relates the precision of a measurement to the lifetime of the system. The rest mass \(m\) is related to the rest energy \(E\) by \(E = mc^2\), so the uncertainty in the rest energy \(dE\) is given by:

\[
dE = \frac{hc}{\tau}
\]

Substituting the given values:
- \(h = 6.6 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}\) (Planck's constant),
- \(c = 3 \times 10^8 \text{ m/s}\) (speed of light),
- \(\tau = 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}\) (lifetime),

\[
dE = \frac{(6.6 \times 10^{-34})(3 \times 10^8)}{2.0 \times 10^{-23}} = 1.1 \times 10^{-13} \text{ J}
\]

Converting to MeV:
\[
dE = \frac{1.1 \times 10^{-13}}{1.6 \times 10^{-13}} \text{ MeV} = 1.1 \times 10^{-13} \text{ MeV}
\]

Thus, the typical range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy is \(\pm 1.1 \times 10^{-13} \text{ MeV}\).
